 "enrollment": 173,
 "briefSummary": "Hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy (HIE) affects approximately 4,000 to 12,000 persons annually in the United States. Mortality from HIE has been reported up to 60%, with at least 25% of survivors left with significant neurocognitive disability. Despite this vital unmet medical need, no pharmacological adjunct or alternative therapy has proven beneficial in improving outcomes in neonatal HIE.\n\nRLS-0071 is a novel peptide being developed for the treatment of neonatal HIE. This study is designed to evaluate the efficacy, safety, and tolerability of RLS-0071 in the treatment of newborns with moderate or severe HIE.",
 "detailedDescription": "This is a Phase 2/3, two-stage, multisite, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study to assess the safety, efficacy, and pharmacokinetics (PK) of RLS-0071 in newborns with moderate or severe HIE undergoing therapeutic hypothermia, with long-term follow-up of up to 24 months of age.\n\nThe study consists of 2 sequential stages for all participants in all cohorts. In Stage 1, participants will receive either ascending doses of RLS-0071 or a matched volume of placebo every 8 hours (q8h) for 72 hours in addition to standard of care treatment, including therapeutic hypothermia. During and after the dosing period, participants will be monitored and assessed through Day 14, including magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) measurements of brain injury. After completion of Stage 1, participants will transition to Stage 2 of the study for long-term observation, including developmental assessments, until participants reach 24 months of age.\n\nThe Phase 2 portion of the STAR study is designed to inform dose escalation and correlation analysis to affirm that MRI measurement of brain injury in HIE babies is predictive of long-term neurocognitive outcomes at 24 months. The Phase 2 portion of the study consists of Cohort 1 and Cohort 2 subsets. The first recruited cohorts, consisting of Cohort 1a (moderate HIE) and 1b (severe HIE), will receive a dose of 3 mg/kg RLS-0071 or a matched volume of placebo. A Data Safety Monitoring Board (DSMB) will review available clinical safety and PK data from Cohort 1 subsets with completed study intervention, and make a recommendation on whether to escalate the dose for moderate and severe HIE cohorts. The Sponsor will consider the DSMB recommendation to make their decision on dose escalation in addition to their own evaluation of all available safety and PK data. If the decision is made to escalate, Cohort 2 subsets (2a \\[moderate\\] and 2b \\[severe\\]) will be recruited to receive an escalated dose of RLS-0071 (10 mg/kg) or a matched volume of placebo. Following the completion of study intervention for each Cohort 2 subset (2a \\[moderate\\] or 2b \\[severe\\]), the DSMB will review available safety and PK data and make a recommendation whether to expand enrollment for Cohort 2+.\n\nThe Phase 3 portion of the study is designed to generate data focusing on assessing the efficacy of RLS-0071 compared to placebo in reducing HIE brain injury as measured by MRI and improving the long-term neurocognitive outcomes at 24 months. The Phase 3 portion of the study consists of Cohort 2+ (2a+ \\[moderate\\] or 2b+ \\[severe\\]) and Cohort 3 (3a \\[moderate\\] or 3b \\[severe\\]), with participants recruited in these cohorts receiving 10 mg/kg RLS-0071 or a matched volume of placebo.",
 "arms": [
  {
   "label": "RLS-0071",
   "type": "EXPERIMENTAL",
   "description": "Doses of RLS-0071 to be administered every 8 hours (q8h), for a total of 10 doses over 72 hours.",
   "interventionNames": [
    "Drug: RLS-0071"
   ]
  },
  {
   "label": "Placebo",
   "type": "PLACEBO_COMPARATOR",
