 "enrollment": 210,
 "briefSummary": "The goal of this clinical trial is to evaluate whether DLBS1442 can alleviate primary dysmenorrhea in young adults aged 18 to 21 years. The main question it aims to answer is:\n\n1\\. Does DLBS1442, at doses of 100 mg and 200 mg, reduce dysmenorrhea pain compared with placebo, as measured by the change from baseline in Visual Analogue Scale (VAS) pain score at predefined time points after the first dose?\n\nResearchers will compare DLBS1442 100 mg, DLBS1442 200 mg, with placebo to determine whether DLBS1442 is more effective in reducing dysmenorrhea pain.\n\nParticipants will:\n\n1. Take DLBS1442 or placebo three times daily for up to 5 consecutive days during one menstrual cycle. Treatment may be discontinued earlier if pain completely resolves (VAS = 0).\n2. Return to the study site on Day 6 after Visit 1 for assessment of study drug compliance, diary review, return of unused study medication and packaging, and study-related evaluations.\n3. Record the Visual Analogue Scale (VAS) pain score before each study drug administration.\n4. Complete the quality-of-life questionnaire on Days 1, 2, 3, and 6.\n5. Record any adverse events and concomitant medications used throughout the study.\n6. Record the use of rescue medication (paracetamol), including the VAS pain score immediately before each rescue medication dose.",
 "detailedDescription": "Primary dysmenorrhea is a prevalent gynecological condition affecting adolescents and young women and is characterized by recurrent menstrual pain in the absence of identifiable pelvic pathology. Although nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs are considered the standard treatment, some patients experience inadequate pain relief or are unable to tolerate these medications. Therefore, the development of alternative therapeutic options with favorable efficacy and safety profiles remains clinically important.\n\nDLBS1442 is a standardized bioactive fraction derived from Phaleria macrocarpa (Mahkota Dewa), an Indonesian medicinal plant. Pharmacological studies have demonstrated anti-inflammatory and immunomodulatory properties of DLBS1442, suggesting its potential to reduce menstrual pain associated with inflammatory pathways in primary dysmenorrhea.\n\nThis is a Phase II/III, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-group, dose-ranging clinical trial designed to evaluate the efficacy and safety of DLBS1442 in female participants aged 18 to 21 years with primary dysmenorrhea. Eligible participants will be randomized in a 1:1:1 ratio to receive DLBS1442 100 mg, DLBS1442 200 mg, or matching placebo for up to 5 consecutive days during one menstrual cycle. Study treatment may be discontinued before Day 5 if complete pain relief (VAS = 0) is achieved, while study assessments will continue according to the protocol.\n\nEfficacy will be evaluated using serial assessments of menstrual pain intensity with the Visual Analogue Scale (VAS) following the first dose and throughout the treatment period. Health-related quality of life will be evaluated using the EQ-5D-5L questionnaire. Rescue medication (paracetamol) will be permitted according to predefined protocol criteria and documented throughout the study. Safety evaluations will include monitoring of adverse events, concomitant medication use, physical examinations, and laboratory assessments in participants with a history of hepatic or renal impairment.\n\nThis study is designed to characterize the dose-response relationship of DLBS1442 and to provide evidence regarding its efficacy and safety as a potential treatment for primary dysmenorrhea.",
